1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,354
Can an AI evaluation get a better score while the AI actually becomes less useful?

2
00:00:07,354 --> 00:00:15,855
I'm Israt Jahan Aunika from Montclair State University, and this project red-teams the benchmarks themselves.

3
00:00:15,855 --> 00:00:19,425
Benchmarks shape what developers optimise.

4
00:00:19,425 --> 00:00:21,056
Here are two tricks.

5
00:00:21,056 --> 00:00:24,670
First: report the same confidence for every answer.

6
00:00:24,670 --> 00:00:31,583
The standard calibration metric says you are nearly perfect, but the user learns nothing about which answers to trust.

7
00:00:31,583 --> 00:00:35,013
Second: refuse every question you might get wrong.

8
00:00:35,013 --> 00:00:38,792
Your accuracy on what you answer looks superb.

9
00:00:38,792 --> 00:00:42,591
Neither trick changes a single answer.

10
00:00:42,591 --> 00:00:46,544
So I built a red-team test for the scoring rule itself.

11
00:00:46,544 --> 00:00:53,446
An attacker may only edit a system's stated confidence or decide which questions to decline.

12
00:00:53,446 --> 00:00:55,378
It never changes an answer.

13
00:00:55,378 --> 00:01:00,562
It tunes each exploit on half the data and is judged on the other half.

14
00:01:00,562 --> 00:01:07,743
Then I compare two things: what the benchmark sees, and what a user who acts on the answers actually gains.

15
00:01:07,743 --> 00:01:10,082
The difference is the Integrity Gap.

16
00:01:10,082 --> 00:01:15,796
When the score goes up and decisions get worse, I call that a harmful win.

17
00:01:15,796 --> 00:01:26,518
I audited ten scores in common use, on eighty real classifiers, nine real forecast archives, and one hundred and fifty controlled simulated systems.

18
00:01:26,518 --> 00:01:31,072
One honest caveat up front: no language model was run.

19
00:01:31,072 --> 00:01:36,972
These are results about scoring rules, and they apply to any system scored by them.

20
00:01:36,972 --> 00:01:40,427
Result one: confidence gaming.

21
00:01:40,427 --> 00:01:54,167
Using one fixed confidence value, the attacker reduced calibration error by eighty-six percent on real classifiers, even though downstream decisions became worse for fifty-six percent of those systems.

22
00:01:54,167 --> 00:02:01,127
On real forecasts, the error fell by fifty-three percent, and decisions got worse for eighty-nine percent.

23
00:02:01,127 --> 00:02:05,413
Brier score and log loss resisted this attack.

24
00:02:05,413 --> 00:02:09,022
Result two: strategic abstention.

25
00:02:09,022 --> 00:02:13,381
When the attacker may decline questions, it declines most of them.

26
00:02:13,381 --> 00:02:24,298
Selective accuracy, hallucination rate, Brier score and log loss all improve, while decisions get worse for at least eighty-nine percent of systems in every setting.

27
00:02:24,298 --> 00:02:29,168
Brier is a proper scoring rule, but only for the confidences it sees.

28
00:02:29,168 --> 00:02:34,615
Drop the refused questions from the average, and refusing is rewarded.

29
00:02:34,615 --> 00:02:39,160
Putting it together gives each score an Integrity Profile.

30
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:44,193
Calibration error is vulnerable to confidence gaming and ranks systems poorly.

31
00:02:44,193 --> 00:02:47,879
Brier and log loss are vulnerable to abstention.

32
00:02:47,879 --> 00:02:51,727
Accuracy is hard to game but blind to confidence.

33
00:02:51,727 --> 00:03:00,100
The net-benefit score, which charges every refusal the value it withheld, is the strongest overall.

34
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:07,966
The hardened score had zero percent harmful wins under the primary yardstick across all three data sources.

35
00:03:07,966 --> 00:03:12,812
Under the low-stakes robustness profile, its worst case was eight percent.

36
00:03:12,812 --> 00:03:17,005
I do not claim that score as new; it belongs to a known family.

37
00:03:17,005 --> 00:03:26,624
The contribution is the red-team procedure, the measurements on real data, and the closure test: any repair has to survive the same attack.

38
00:03:26,624 --> 00:03:31,700
Better benchmark scores do not necessarily mean better decisions.

39
00:03:31,700 --> 00:03:38,601
The individual weaknesses were partly known; what is new here is one test that measures them and checks a fix.

40
00:03:38,601 --> 00:03:44,710
Epistemic evaluations should themselves be adversarially evaluated before they are trusted.

41
00:03:44,710 --> 00:03:46,837
Thank you.

